  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Lafiya &#x2013; Health Resilience of North East Nigeria (HeRON)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Nigeria):  With FY 2022 funds this activity will:.Support at least 83 health facilities and seven nutrition stabilization centers across 23 LGAs (13 in Borno and 10 in Yobe). .Focus on integration of quality of care into the basic Primary Health Care package to ensure that basic package of services is available and accessible;.Procure essential medicines and supplies while coordinating and leveraging Government of Nigeria and existing donors&apos; investments supporting Malaria, Family Planning, HIV/TB, and nutrition commodities; .Build on the existing pool of trainers established during the past two years, an integrated competency focused on-the-job mentoring and coaching approach will be adopted, with facility-based training in a low-dose high-frequency approach; and .Transition internally displaced persons in select IDP camps in Adamawa state from emergency humanitarian aid dependency to independent self-sustaining residents..Integrate WASH activities into health programming by promoting the adoption of essential WASH actions, food and environmental hygiene including handwashing, and safe disposal of medical waste at the health facility levels.The overall purpose of the Health Resilience in Northeast Nigeria (HeRON) activity (previously known as the Northeast Health Transition Program) is to save lives and reduce suffering for the poorest and most vulnerable in North-East Nigeria, through a stronger and more resilient health system. This activity contributes directly to USAID Nigeria CDCS Development Objective 2, the Embassy&apos;s ICS, and is closely aligned with Goal 3 of the 2022-2026 Joint Strategic Plan...</narrative>
  </description>
